1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,585
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,585 --> 00:00:04,744
Episode 8.

3
00:00:04,744 --> 00:00:07,485
The Dinosaur at the Window.

4
00:00:07,485 --> 00:00:10,511
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:10,511 --> 00:00:14,176
Are all dinosaurs completely gone from Earth?

6
00:00:14,176 --> 00:00:15,767
Ready?

7
00:00:15,767 --> 00:00:17,968
Let us find out.

8
00:00:17,968 --> 00:00:23,196
At the museum, Maya stands under an enormous dinosaur skeleton.

9
00:00:23,196 --> 00:00:25,809
Leo looks at its teeth.

10
00:00:25,809 --> 00:00:29,843
I am glad it does not want my sandwich, he says.

11
00:00:29,843 --> 00:00:32,073
Pip reads a sign.

12
00:00:32,073 --> 00:00:35,014
Some dinosaurs had feathers.

13
00:00:35,014 --> 00:00:36,946
Like a chicken?

14
00:00:36,946 --> 00:00:38,991
asks Maya.

15
00:00:38,991 --> 00:00:41,278
The guide smiles.

16
00:00:41,278 --> 00:00:45,454
Birds belong to a branch of the dinosaur family tree.

17
00:00:45,454 --> 00:00:48,082
They are living dinosaurs.

18
00:00:48,082 --> 00:00:50,823
Leo looks toward the window.

19
00:00:50,823 --> 00:00:53,877
A small bird is sitting outside.

20
00:00:53,877 --> 00:00:56,008
That little bird?

21
00:00:56,008 --> 00:00:58,607
Yes, says the guide.

22
00:00:58,607 --> 00:01:01,732
Not every dinosaur was huge.

23
00:01:01,732 --> 00:01:07,585
Scientists compare fossils and living animals to understand their relationships.

24
00:01:07,585 --> 00:01:12,400
Pip imagines the little bird carrying the giant skeleton away.

25
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:16,278
His screen displays a warning: Size mismatch.

26
00:01:16,278 --> 00:01:21,136
Being related does not mean being the same size, Maya tells him.

27
00:01:21,136 --> 00:01:25,440
The guide shows them a fossil with marks left by feathers.

28
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:31,661
They cannot hear the animal or see it move, but the fossil preserves clues from long ago.

29
00:01:31,661 --> 00:01:34,801
Did this dinosaur sound like a duck?

30
00:01:34,801 --> 00:01:37,017
Leo asks.

31
00:01:37,017 --> 00:01:40,923
That is an interesting question, says the guide.

32
00:01:40,923 --> 00:01:45,440
We cannot know every ancient sound just by looking at bones.

33
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:48,579
Outside, the little bird chirps.

34
00:01:48,579 --> 00:01:51,676
Leo holds his sandwich close.

35
00:01:51,676 --> 00:01:57,088
I respect your family history, he tells the bird, but this lunch is mine.

36
00:01:57,088 --> 00:02:04,843
Pip writes in his museum notebook: Today we met a giant dinosaur indoors and a very small living one outdoors.

37
00:02:04,843 --> 00:02:07,997
Now, a little more to discover.

38
00:02:07,997 --> 00:02:11,974
Modern birds evolved within a group of dinosaurs.

39
00:02:11,974 --> 00:02:17,627
Fossils can preserve bones, footprints, and sometimes impressions of feathers.

40
00:02:17,627 --> 00:02:23,323
They provide evidence about ancient life, but they do not answer every question.

41
00:02:23,323 --> 00:02:28,650
Scientists separate what evidence supports from what remains uncertain.

42
00:02:28,650 --> 00:02:31,818
Let us hear the main idea again.

43
00:02:31,818 --> 00:02:37,059
The museum visit connected a huge skeleton with a small bird outside.

44
00:02:37,059 --> 00:02:40,639
Birds are part of the dinosaur family tree.

45
00:02:40,639 --> 00:02:49,531
Fossils helped scientists discover that relationship, while some questions, such as many ancient sounds, remain difficult to answer.

46
00:02:49,531 --> 00:02:52,670
Here are three useful expressions.

47
00:02:52,670 --> 00:02:55,852
You can listen, or say them with me.

48
00:02:55,852 --> 00:02:58,480
What was it like long ago?

49
00:02:58,480 --> 00:03:01,107
What was it like long ago?

50
00:03:01,107 --> 00:03:04,375
They are related, but they are different.

51
00:03:04,375 --> 00:03:07,642
They are related, but they are different.

52
00:03:07,642 --> 00:03:10,184
We need more evidence.

53
00:03:10,184 --> 00:03:12,727
We need more evidence.

54
00:03:12,727 --> 00:03:15,724
Remember our listening question?

55
00:03:15,724 --> 00:03:19,389
Are all dinosaurs completely gone from Earth?

56
00:03:19,389 --> 00:03:21,676
Here is the answer.

57
00:03:21,676 --> 00:03:23,338
No.

58
00:03:23,338 --> 00:03:30,099
Modern birds are living members of the dinosaur family tree, although the other dinosaur groups died out.

59
00:03:30,099 --> 00:03:34,062
And here is a question just for your imagination.

60
00:03:34,062 --> 00:03:37,926
What would you ask a scientist who studies fossils?

61
00:03:37,926 --> 00:03:41,235
There is more than one possible answer.

62
00:03:41,235 --> 00:03:44,531
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
